## Incident 2 — Kind CNI Failure on EC2

### What Happened
Kind (Kubernetes in Docker) failed to install the CNI network plugin on EC2.

```
ERROR: failed to create cluster: failed to apply overlay network
Unable to connect to the server: context deadline exceeded
X Installing CNI
```

### Root Cause
Kind needs privileged network access to configure overlay networking inside Docker containers. EC2 instances restrict this by default — the container inside Docker couldn't configure its own network stack.

### Fix Applied
Switched from Kind to **k3s** — a lightweight Kubernetes distribution that works natively on EC2 without privileged networking requirements.

```bash
# Install k3s (works on EC2 out of the box)
curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -

# Configure kubectl
mkdir -p ~/.kube
sudo cp /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml ~/.kube/config
sudo chown ubuntu:ubuntu ~/.kube/config
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config
echo "export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config" >> ~/.bashrc

# Verify cluster running
kubectl get nodes
# Output: node Ready
```

### What I Learned
| Tool | Best For | Works on EC2? |
|---|---|---|
| Kind | Local dev, CI pipelines | Sometimes — needs privileged networking |
| k3s | Lightweight prod-like clusters | Yes — works perfectly |
| minikube | Local dev only | No — needs VM or Docker driver |

**k3s is the right choice for EC2-based Kubernetes labs.**

